  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 102 PM CDT Fri Oct 2 2026\n\nTHIS AFTERNOON: Mid-lvl clouds associated with increased warm \nadvection on the 700:600 mb layer will continue to spread north and \neast. Satellite showers increasing cloud growth north of I-90, \nso anticipating very light showers to sprinkles will accompany \nthis cloud cover eastward, focused primarily north of I-90 \ntowards Highway 14 (Huron, Brookings, Marshall) into very early \nevening. QPF amounts will remain minimal. \n\nTONIGHT:  Lee side troughing over the western High Plains will begin \nto shift eastward overnight, allowing an increased SPG to keep a \nrather breezy southeasterly wind through the area. Unlike Friday, \nmorning, low temperatures should be much warmer through the region. \nThe highest gusts may be near the higher terrain areas of the \nBuffalo ridge where 30 to 40 mph gusts are possible. \n\nSATURDAY: A surface front slowly crawls east on Saturday, keeping \nbreezy to gusty south and then southwest winds through the CWA. \nSoundings suggest a period of stronger mixing from mid-morning \nthrough early afternoon with a few 40+ mph gusts could be possible \n(highest in the Ridge areas).  With less cloud cover ahead of the \nfront, and deep mixing we should see a rise in temperatures towards \nthe 70s in the afternoon.  The populated NBM sits at the 75th \npercentile, but with the increased mixing and warm low-lvls would \nnot be surprised to see highs a bit warmer and have nudged us \ninto the 90th percentile in a few areas. Have also lowered dew \npoints slightly. The arrival a subtle shortwave by mid afternoon\ncould lead to scattered sprinkles to light showers along and \nnorth of Highway 14 in the afternoon and into SW Minnesota in \nthe evening. QPF will be very light. \n\nSATURDAY NIGHT: The passage of the front will bring slightly \ncooler temperatures southeast overnight. Would not be surprised \nto see patchy fog by Sunday morning as winds weaken. \n\nSUNDAY: Cooler Canadian high pressure arrives for Sunday, featuring \nlight winds and plenty of sunshine. This should be an absolutely \nbeautiful fall day with highs in the mid 60s. \n\nMONDAY-FRIDAY:  A VERY quiet forecast remains for most of next week \nas broad mid-lvl ridging over the CONUS forces stronger jet stream \nenergy north of the Canadian border. With the prevailing flow \nforcing any meaningful Gulf air away from the area, and no ability \nto bring any tropical air from the East Pacific, we're expecting a \ndry week. In fact the latest GEFS/ECE ensembles paint less than a \n20% chance for >0.10\" over the next 10 days in the Tri-State area. \n\nTemperatures will be influenced by the passage of several weak \nfronts through the week. A return of gusty winds on Monday marks the \nfirst dry frontal passage Monday night. A second front passes \nthrough the area by Thursday.  Temperatures are already favored to \nbe warmer than normal through the region, and would not be\nsurprised to see a few of those breezier days even warmer. \n850mb temperature anomalies are expected to be at or above the \n75th percentile through the week, further supporting the arrival\nof an extended period of above normal temperatures into next \nweekend.",
